publica functio f() -> u8 poscit alloc, horologium {
    redde 0;
}
```

```exsecutor
publica functio applica(v: u32, f: functio(u32) -> u32) -> u32 poscit sicut f {
    redde f(v);
}

functio duplica(x: u32) -> u32 {
    redde x * 2;
}

publica functio usus() -> u32 {
    redde applica(21, duplica);
}
```

A capability row is part of a function type. `poscit sicut f` means "the row carried by the type of parameter `f`", substituted positionally at each call site. A caller passing a pure function pays nothing; one passing a function that needs `rete` must declare `rete` itself, which is what makes laundering impossible rather than merely annotated. `[OPEN]`: the call through `f` type-checks and cannot be emitted yet, because indirect calls are a refused opcode (§9.2); measured, `exsc -o` on it terminates with `SIGILL`, so this is checked and not run.

Explain what this Exsecutor does and why it is written this way:

