}
```

```exsecutor
publica functio summa_viginti(v: acies<f32, 20>) -> f32 {
    quisque i in 0..20
        contrahe s: +
        forma arborea 8
    {
        s = v[i];
    }
    redde s;
}
```

`forma arborea 8` declares the tree shape as part of the operation: groups of 8 in index order, the last group holding the remainder (20 elements are `[0..8) [8..16) [16..20)`), pairwise inside a group, folded left to right across groups. Floating-point addition is not associative, so the shape is semantics, not an optimization. `+` is one of the associative operators `contrahe` accepts.

Explain what this Exsecutor does and why it is written this way:

```exsecutor
publica functio summa_viginti(v: acies<f32, 20>) -> f32 {
    quisque i in 0..20
        contrahe s: +
        forma arborea 8
    {
        s = v[i];
    }
    redde s;
}
```

```exsecutor
publica functio summa_octo(v: acies<u64, 8>) -> u64 {
    quisque i in 0..8
        contrahe s: +
        forma ordinata
    {
        s = v[i];
    }
    redde s;
}
```

`quisque` declares that iterations do not observe one another. `contrahe s: +` names the accumulator and its operator, and `forma ordinata` fixes the reduction shape to strictly left to right, so the result is reproducible whatever the machine does. Inside the body, `s = v[i];` is the *contribution*, not an assignment of the total, and the running `s` may not be read there (that is EXS-E0341). After the loop `s` is an ordinary binding. Because there is no dependency analysis yet, `quisque` is today a trusted assertion `[OPEN]` (§8.5).

Explain what this Exsecutor does and why it is written this way:

```exsecutor
publica functio summa_octo(v: acies<u64, 8>) -> u64 {
    quisque i in 0..8
        contrahe s: +
        forma ordinata
    {
        s = v[i];
    }
    redde s;
}
```

```exsecutor
publica functio primum_par(n: u64) -> u64 {
    mutabilis r: u64 = 0;
    per i in 0..n {
        si i residuum 2 ne 0 {
            perge;
        }
        r = i;
        rumpe;
    }
    redde r;
}
```

`perge` continues to the next iteration and `rumpe` leaves the loop; both are imperatives, as `redde` is (§8.5, "Mood"). `residuum` is integer remainder, one of the contextual words — it is an operator only in operator position. `rumpe` is forbidden inside an iteration carrying a `contrahe` (EXS-E0342); the spec says nothing against `perge`, and `exsc` accepts it there.

Explain what this Exsecutor does and why it is written this way:

```exsecutor
publica functio primum_par(n: u64) -> u64 {
    mutabilis r: u64 = 0;
    per i in 0..n {
        si i residuum 2 ne 0 {
            perge;
        }
        r = i;
        rumpe;
    }
    redde r;
}
```

```exsecutor
publica functio maximum(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {
    si a gt b {
        redde a;
    }
    redde b;
}
```

`si` opens the condition and `redde` gives the value back. The comparison is the word `gt`, never `>`: `<` and `>` only ever delimit generic arguments (§8.6 decision 6). The block after `si` returns, so control only reaches the final `redde` when `a gt b` was false. `publica` makes the function part of the module's interface; with no `poscit` its declared row is empty, so it can touch nothing but its arguments.

Explain what this Exsecutor does and why it is written this way:

```exsecutor
publica functio maximum(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {
    si a gt b {
        redde a;
    }
    redde b;
}
```

```exsecutor
publica functio signum(x: i32) -> i32 {
    si x lt 0 {
        redde -1;
    } sin x eq 0 {
        redde 0;
    } aliter {
        redde 1;
    }
}
```

`si … sin … aliter` is the whole conditional ladder. `sin` is classical Latin for "but if" and takes the place of `else if` / `elif`; `aliter` ("otherwise") is the final branch. Every branch ends in `redde`, so no join is needed after the ladder.

